---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';

const eyesUrl = 'https://eyes.nasa.gov/apps/solar-system/#/home';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="NASA's Eyes on the Solar System"
  description="Explore a real-time, interactive 3D model of the solar system with NASA's Eyes on the Solar System — recommended by the Phoenix Astronomical Society."
>
  <Hero
    image="/images/placeholder-hero-events.svg"
    alt="Placeholder — real member astrophotography will replace this image"
    eyebrow="Explore"
    title="NASA's Eyes on the Solar System"
    subtitle="A free, real-time 3D visualization of the solar system, built by NASA/JPL — see where every planet, moon, and spacecraft is right now."
  >
    <a href={eyesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn-primary mt-8">
      Launch Eyes on the Solar System ↗
    </a>
  </Hero>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-20">
    <h2 class="section-heading">What It Is</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-ink-muted">
      Eyes on the Solar System is a free, browser-based 3D visualization tool from NASA's Jet Propulsion
      Laboratory. It plots the real, current positions of the planets, moons, comets, asteroids, and active
      spacecraft using actual NASA mission data — you can fly through the solar system, fast-forward or
      rewind time, and follow individual missions along their real trajectories.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink-muted">
      It's a great companion to naked-eye and telescope observing: use it to see why a planet is or isn't
      visible tonight, track where a spacecraft like Voyager or Perseverance is right now, or just get a
      feel for the actual scale and motion of the solar system.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-8">
      <a
        href={eyesUrl}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="text-nebula-teal hover:underline"
      >
        Open NASA's Eyes on the Solar System ↗</a
      >
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-muted">
      It's a full 3D app best experienced on its own page, so this link opens it directly on eyes.nasa.gov.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-subtle">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 text-center">
      <a href="/sky-conditions" class="btn-secondary">← Back to Sky Conditions</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
